{{also|Cadence|cadencé}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=cadence}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkeɪ.dn̩s/|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|cadence|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|cadenza|lang=it}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|cadentia|lang=la}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or state of declining or sinking.
#* Milton
#*: Now was the sun in western '''cadence''' low.
# Balanced, rhythmic flow.
#* Shakespeare
#*: golden '''cadence''' of poesy
#* {{quote-news|year=1991|date=2 December|title=At the Saudi-Kuwaiti Border|work=ABC Nightline|passage=Night has now passed in the Saudi desert and as we hear from Nightline correspondent Forrest Sawyer, the normal '''cadence''' of life at the front is about to change.}}
# The measure or beat of movement.
#* {{quote-journal|year=1993|volume=98|issue=5|page=102|title=Terror of the deep|author=Ken Schultz|journal=Field and Stream|passage=Getting into a good jigging rhythm means making short quick jerks in a regular '''cadence''' that might average about one jerk every 1.5 to 2 seconds.}}
# The general inflection or modulation of the voice, or of any sound.
#* Milton
#*: Blustering winds, which all night long / Had roused the sea, now with hoarse '''cadence''' lull / Seafaring men o'erwatched.
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: The accents {{...}} were in passion's tenderest '''cadence'''.
#* {{quote-news|date=30 December|year=1991|title=Raimey: A Breath of Fresh Ayah|author=David Holmstrom|work=Christian Science Monitor|passage=The '''cadence''' of Raimey's voice is pure Down-Easter Maine}}
# {{context|music|lang=en}} A progression of at least two [[chord|chords]] which conclude a piece of [[music]], section or musical phrases within it. Sometimes referred to [[analogy|analogously]] as musical [[punctuation]].
# {{context|music|lang=en}} A [[cadenza]], or closing embellishment; a pause before the end of a [[strain]], which the performer may fill with a [[flight of fancy]].
# {{context|speech|lang=en}} A fall in [[inflection]] of a speaker’s voice, such as at the end of a sentence.
# {{context|dance|lang=en}} A dance move which ends a phrase.
#: ''The '''cadence''' in a galliard step refers to the final leap in a cinquepace sequence.''
# {{context|fencing|lang=en}} The rhythm and sequence of a series of actions.
# {{context|running|lang=en}} The number of steps per minute.
# {{context|cycling|lang=en}} The number of revolutions per minute of the [[crank]]s or pedals of a [[bicycle]].
# {{context|military|lang=en}} A chant that is sung by military personnel while running or marching; a [[jody call]].
# {{cx|heraldry|lang=en}} [[cadency]]
# {{cx|horse-riding|lang=en}} Harmony and proportion of movement, as in a well-managed [[horse]].

